  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (like lisinopril) and ARBs (like losartan) are top-tier, first-line choices for treating high blood pressure. They lower blood pressure by targeting the same hormonal pathway in your body, but they do it at different steps.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ACE inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/hypertension/ace-inhibitors-vs-arbs-whats-the-difference"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (like lisinopril) and ARBs (like losartan) are top-tier, first-line choices for treating high blood pressure"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Here is how they compare when you are starting fresh with treatment."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Equally good at lowering blood pressure: Large studies show that ARBs and ACE inhibitors work just as well as each other to prevent heart attacks, strokes, and heart failure."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The outcome is the same: Neither class shows a major superiority over the other when it comes to protecting your heart or kidneys as a first-line choice."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Safety"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs are safer and better tolerated: Large-scale real-world data shows that patients taking ARBs have a significantly lower risk of side effects."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The cough factor: ACE inhibitors often cause a nagging, dry cough because they lead to a buildup of a substance called bradykinin. ARBs do not cause this cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: ACE inhibitors carry a small but serious risk of angioedema (sudden, deep swelling of the face, lips, or tongue), which is much rarer with ARBs."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared risks: Both classes can raise potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and both must be avoided during pregnancy due to severe risks to the baby."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost and Availability"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Affordable generics: Both drug classes have been around for a long time. Most options in both groups are available as low-cost generic medications."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Bottom Line"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Medical guidelines historically recommended ACE inhibitors first simply because they were studied earlier. However, newer large-scale evidence suggests that ARBs should often be preferred when starting treatment because they offer the same blood pressure control with far fewer bothersome side effects."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you want, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you starting a new medication or considering switching from an ACE inhibitor to an ARB?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any specific side effects or other health conditions (like kidney disease or diabetes)?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can give you more tailored information to discuss with your doctor."
      }
    ],
